{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfkeEBKpBFbkqmfiirrvj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate, `lempel_ziv_novelty_parse`, and it passes the self-check (`OK`). The check didn't test convergence or novelty, and this model will almost certainly lose to the current best, as this \"simpler or higher-variance\" slot invites.\n\n**Hypothesis:** People judge how random a sequence looks by how compressible it is when read once from left to right. They break it into \"phrases\" (Lempel-Ziv parsing), where each phrase is the shortest stretch that hasn't appeared earlier in the sequence. A sequence that keeps producing new phrases looks random. One that keeps repeating earlier material looks designed: streaks, alternation and repeated motifs. Each sequence's phrase count is compared with what a fair coin typically gives at that length, and the sequence with the higher relative count is chosen. Each person has their own decisiveness and their own habit of favouring one side.\n\n**How the model is built:**\n- One stimulus feature: the difference between the two sequences' standardised phrase counts. The fair-coin mean and spread come from listing every possible sequence of that length.\n- Person-level decisiveness and side habit, each drawn from a population, with 400 slots so new participants can be predicted.\n- No other cues are mixed in, and none of the earlier \"fair coin versus regular generator\" machinery. None of the 68 earlier attempts used compression-based randomness.\n\n**What it predicts that others don't:** All length-2 pairs come out at chance (HH and HT both parse into 2 phrases), and length-3 pairs are only weakly separated. This is a sharp, testable claim about the short-pair critique, which found people prefer the more-switching sequence in short pairs less than the current best model predicts. The model also rates perfect alternation as fairly regular: HTHTHTHT scores −1.34 against the fair-coin average, and HHHHHHHH −2.85.\n\n**Check results:**\n- Smoke-fit ELPD-LOO was −4664.5 (100 draws, 1 chain). The best model on this data is at −3568.2.\n- R-hat couldn't be computed, because a single chain can't report one.\n- PSIS-LOO was flagged unreliable: 3.1% of trials had Pareto k above 0.5, against a 1% tolerance. That was on the short fit, so admission's full fit may or may not clear it.\n\nFiles are in `experiment3/model_loop/iter_2/candidate_2/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1144,"cache_read_input_tokens":80305,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1144,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1ca4e0b6-9094-4f24-90c5-01cb22a6786a","uuid":"0907cd19-e6ec-4487-920d-fed25ee96a8c","timestamp":"2026-10-06T08:09:19.235Z","request_id":"req_011CfkeEAqoSZjRXEfdUhVRy"}
